\section{The actual startup-to-Live proof obligation}
\label{sec:study-hybrid-plan}

\subsection{A measurement-only observer, not an already running QMEKF}
\label{sec:vertical-proxy}
For orientation, the ideal continuous Mahony proxy has the form
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \varepsilon&=\hat d_{\rm meas}\times\hat d(\hat q_P),
 &\hat d_{\rm meas}&=-f^B/\|f^B\|,\\
 \dot{\hat q}_P&=\tfrac12\hat q_P\otimes
       (\omega^B+b_P+k_P\varepsilon),
 &\dot b_P&=k_I\varepsilon .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:proxy-mahony}
\end{equation}
The rate vector is understood as a pure-vector quaternion. The proxy integral
$b_P$ is distinct from the physical accelerometer bias $\beta$ used in the finite-word analysis.
The gains are $k_P=0.1\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and
$k_I=0.01\,\mathrm{s^{-2}}$ (the implementation stores doubled gains).
Its attitude levels the measurement-only up-acceleration channel,
\begin{equation}
 a^P_\uparrow=-\bigl([R_{NB}(\hat q_P)f^B]_D+g\bigr).
\end{equation}
That channel feeds the period estimator, bandpass, statistics, stillness logic
and tuner without feedback from the main MEKF. No independent front-end
restart is allowed at a proof-window boundary.

Equation~\eqref{eq:proxy-mahony} motivates the continuous observer lemmas
\cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary}; it does not identify the shipping
binary32 Euler/normalization recurrence with a continuous flow. The actual
initialization, nonlinear normalization branches, finite precision and wrapper
gates require their own correspondence and reachability arguments.

\subsection{Stages and events that must be carried}
The execution sequence begins with construction and accelerometer-based
initialization of the private observer. Cold/TunerWarm carry the gravity
average, period/band/statistics state, tuner candidates, applied values and
clocks. An actual \texttt{goLive} commits the current parameters, initializes
the full covariance and latent-acceleration block, sets the one Live origin,
and starts with accelerometer-bias learning held (H18). The frontend memories
continue; they are not synthetic seeds selected for the proof.

Normal Live includes prediction, due $S=0$ updates, accelerometer updates,
magnetic calls and their accepted/rejected branches, staged tuner commits and
covariance maintenance. H18/A21 hold/release transitions, initial heading
acquisition, later reference refinement, continuous hard-iron application and
tilt-watchdog resets are additional hybrid events. The carried covariance is
$21\times21$ even in H18. Its bias uncertainty still affects accelerometer
innovation covariance. A hold does not make the true bias zero.

The detailed implementation gates are retained in
Sec.~\ref{sec:startup-policy}. For example the ordinary startup path includes
minimum warmup and sustained alignment, angular-rate and readiness tests. A
configured timeout is still guarded; merely reaching its clock value neither
forces Live nor proves that the true error is small. Each handoff must be
mapped into a predeclared entry domain using the actual physical state and
stored covariance, not an assumed covariance-consistency ellipsoid.
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-contraction-handover.md}
\studysource{tools/stability/ou3_alt_contraction/finite_startup_live_runtime_bridge.py}
\studysource{tools/stability/ou3_alt_contraction/finite_startup_first_live_step.py}

\subsection{The theorem has several logically separate stages}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
\item \textbf{Admissible execution and startup.} Bind every sensor packet,
physical increment and BIAS history to construction; establish safe arithmetic
and actual progression through the wrapper, or state a separate capture
hypothesis. An auxiliary Mahony basin is not yet a shipping reachability proof.
\item \textbf{Fresh Live entry.} Derive attitude, motion and bias error, full
covariance, committed $R_S$, OU parameters, frontend/calibration state and
clocks from that same execution. H18 is the real initial mode; no independent
Live state or favorable metric is installed.
\item \textbf{Finite Live dynamics.} Compose all literal IMU, magnetic and
control events, including skips, repairs, holds, resets and reference changes.
Every prefix must remain in the arithmetic and coordinate domain needed by
the subsequent argument.
\item \textbf{Information-dependent dissipation.} Prove gravity-quotient and
centre behavior during ungauged intervals, and finite-error dissipation over
accepted informative magnetic-service superwords. Endpoint contraction needs
compatible storage and bounded forcing, not just linear observability.
\item \textbf{Retention and conclusions.} Prove entry into and retention of
the claimed domain, including all intermediate times and jumps. Only then
iterate the inequality to obtain the intended ultimate bound and, where
justified, convergence in the zero-total-supply case.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Universal startup versus history-dependent capture}
The proposed universal deadline of 30,002 samples has been falsified by an
admitted native-wrapper history whose first Live sample is 33,447. Recovery of
that history does not prove eventual startup for every other history. A reached
antialigned docking state and a candidate non-Live tail are also retained;
the proposed infinite-tail invariant still depends on unaudited rounding
budgets and indefinite guard/gate/clock composition. A finite replay proves
neither an infinite trap nor universal escape. The domain must not be narrowed
just to delete this witness.

There are two distinct possible theorem targets. The original target requires
\emph{proving} finite startup/capture for every admitted history. The optional
weaker target explicitly \emph{assumes} that, for each history $h$ and allowed
initial state, some finite $T(h,x_0)$ enters a specified retained tail domain.
It need not bound $\sup_{h,x_0}T(h,x_0)$. The tail domain and storage must be
specified before this assumption; ``eventually reaches a stable trajectory''
is not a useful premise. The weaker conclusion is conditional history-wise
eventual practical boundedness, not uniform ISS, a common accuracy guarantee,
or a maximum startup time. Tail dissipation and prefix retention still have
to be proved. Both targets remain available; neither is silently substituted
for the other.
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-startup-timeout-witness.md}
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-startup-trap.md}
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-nonuniform-capture-theorem.md}

\section{Complete finite-word stability formulation}
\label{sec:study-alt-choice}

A stability proof based on independently bounded local updates can lose the
correlations among covariance, gain, measurement geometry, physical forcing,
and hybrid mode. The present approach therefore starts from the controlling
\emph{whole-word} inequality and
uses exact finite descriptors, inverse-free innovation relations and quadratic
constraints. A word is a finite sequence of literal events with all their
predecessors; a superword contains enough such events to state an information
or dissipation condition. The word length is selected to contain the observation events required by the
information condition. Consecutive words share one continuous physical and
estimator trajectory.

The finite error coordinates are
\begin{equation}
 z=(c,e_{bg},e_v,e_p,e_S,e_{aw},e_{ba},\beta)\in\mathbb R^{24}.
 \label{eq:study-joint24}
\end{equation}
The additional three coordinates are the true physical accelerometer bias,
needed to retain its mismatch with the estimator prior. The accompanying state $\xi$ contains the full covariance, physical source,
initialization memory, calibration variables, and hybrid mode. The map is $z^+=F_w(z,\xi,d)$ on a single qualified source, not a product
of independently selected gains. The canonical supplied coordinates
$[e_{ba},\beta]$ remain present in the inequality; their justified bounds do
not license erasing their output rows or endogenous correction paths.

\subsection{Why the old ungauged contraction target was rejected}
\label{sec:study-ungauged}
For a legal quiet ungauged word the heading/axial gyro-bias pair contains the
exact centre block, up to the sign convention for bias error,
\begin{equation}
 \begin{bmatrix}\theta_z^+\\e_{bg,z}^+\end{bmatrix}
 =\begin{bmatrix}1&T\\0&1\end{bmatrix}
  \begin{bmatrix}\theta_z\\e_{bg,z}\end{bmatrix}.
 \label{eq:study-unipotent}
\end{equation}
Its eigenvalue is one and its repeated action has linear heading growth when
axial bias is nonzero. In particular an eigenvector with eigenvalue one would
have unchanged energy in any fixed positive-definite quadratic metric, so no
strict coercive contraction with $\rho<1$ can hold on every such word with
only the declared accelerometer-bias supplies. This is a structural
obstruction, not an interval-resolution problem.

Removing heading alone leaves neutral axial gyro bias. That bias is not a
second physical heading gauge and cannot simply be discarded or renamed an
external disturbance. The selected replacement is therefore
\begin{center}
\emph{gravity quotient plus centre dynamics when ungauged,}\\
\emph{full dissipativity under accepted informative magnetic service.}
\end{center}
A quotient is legitimate only if the actual map descends to it. For a linear
point representation with fibre frame $N$ and transverse frame $Q$, the test
$Q_{\rm after}^{\top}A_wN_{\rm before}=0$ is necessary for the proposed fibre
removal. Its finite nonlinear counterpart must also be established. Quiet-word
zero leakage does not prove this for moving histories. Centre growth and its
coupling into the transverse motion remain explicit obligations.
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-observability-formulation.md}
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-word-rho-feasibility.md}

\subsection{Why not fit a metric or tighten every local bound first?}
A favorable metric chosen independently for each word proves no consistent
stability statement. Likewise, more subdivision around an infeasible master
inequality cannot remove a genuinely unobservable centre direction. The
research protocol first checks complete-word feasibility with preserved
structure, identifies the limiting direction, and distinguishes theorem
failure from a pessimistic enclosure or numerical conditioning failure.
Only a quantitative argument that the remaining enclosure cost can cross
the required margin justifies further certification work. This is the reason
for retaining both exact negative controls and non-promoting diagnostics.
\studysource{AGENTS.md}

\section{Analytical ingredients and where they enter}
\label{sec:study-analytic}

\subsection{Bounded primitives and physical forcing}
If a qualified potential satisfies $p_m=\dot\phi$, the fundamental theorem of
calculus gives $\int_u^t p_m=\phi(t)-\phi(u)$, hence the hard diameter bound.
This simple lemma is needed because the $S=0$ update regularizes an estimator
state even though the true physical integral is generally not zero. With
$e_S=S_L-\hat S$, its actual innovation is
\begin{equation}
 r_S=-\hat S=e_S-S_L .
 \label{eq:study-s-innovation}
\end{equation}
The physical $S_L$ term is a structured forcing channel with the same primitive
history, not an independently chosen disturbance at every event. Omitting it
would falsely turn the motion model into an exact zero-error equilibrium.
The triangle-bound bias lemma \eqref{eq:study-bias-compactness} plays a similar
role for bounded bias coupling; it does not make that coupling disappear.

\subsection{Finite attitude geometry rather than a Taylor replacement}
In one Cayley chart let $c=2\tan(\theta/2)u$ and
$E=R_{\rm true}R_{\rm hat}^{\top}$. Set
\begin{equation}
 C=[c]_\times,\quad D=4+c^{\top}c,\quad
 U=4I+2C+cc^{\top},\quad T_c=U/D .
\end{equation}
Using $C^2=cc^{\top}-(c^{\top}c)I$ gives the exact identities
\begin{equation}
 (I-C/2)U=DI,\qquad E-I=T_cC,
 \qquad (E-I)f=-T_c[f]_\times c .
 \label{eq:study-cayley}
\end{equation}
Thus the finite accelerometer innovation can be written
\begin{equation}
 r_a=-T_c[\hat f]_\times c+E R_{\rm hat}e_{aw}+e_{ba}+\nu_a .
 \label{eq:study-finite-acc}
\end{equation}
The coefficient of physical bias error is $I$ in \emph{both} H18 and A21.
A magnetic residual has the analogous finite attitude factor plus the actual
learned-reference/calibration discrepancy. These are secant identities, not
the linearized $H$ used by the filter to compute its gain. Keeping that
distinction avoids claiming that a product of point Jacobians is the finite
error map. Exact quaternion injection retains the deployed polynomial and
axis-angle branches, normalization, and reset/projection ordering. A
four-chart atlas covers nonzero relative quaternions; chart changes and a
nonzero quaternion lower shell remain runtime obligations rather than a
hidden small-angle entry assumption.
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-finite-measurement-proof.md}
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-attitude-atlas.md}

\subsection{Inverse-free innovation and covariance relations}
On an accepted positive-innovation branch, retain
\begin{equation}
 \Sigma=HPH^{\top}+R,\qquad K\Sigma=N,
 \qquad \Sigma q=r,\quad\delta=Nq .
 \label{eq:study-inverse-free}
\end{equation}
$N$ contains the actual gain masks; it is not always the unmasked $PH^{\top}$.
The relations avoid enclosing a freely varying inverse and preserve the
correlation between covariance, measurement geometry and gain. Rejected or regularized measurement branches are treated as distinct hybrid
events with their corresponding operands. The covariance polynomial on the stated branch is
\begin{equation}
 P^+=P-KN^{\top}-NK^{\top}+K\Sigma K^{\top},
\end{equation}
followed by the corresponding reset and projection maps. Covariance
positive-definiteness must be preserved by the complete hybrid update.

Conditional on the held block invariant $P=\operatorname{diag}(P_{18},B_0)$,
block multiplication yields
\begin{equation}
 \Sigma_H=H_{18}P_{18}H_{18}^{\top}+R_a+B_0,
 \qquad N_H=\begin{bmatrix}P_{18}H_{18}^{\top}\\0\end{bmatrix} .
\end{equation}
This rank-three innovation structure can reduce calculation cost without
losing the full covariance. It is a covariance reduction, not permission to
remove $e_{ba}$ from the finite residual or active-bias output energy from the
storage. A reduced kernel using bare $R_a$ would describe a different update.
Likewise, Euclidean nonexpansiveness of projection onto a ball containing truth
is only a local ingredient; it need not contract a coupled $P^{-1}$ norm.

\subsection{What quadratic constraints and linear lemmas contribute}
Bounds such as $\|w\|^2\le W^2$ can be written as quadratic constraints after
appending a constant coordinate. Sector relations, exact polynomial identities
and masked gain equations restrict the feasible operands of the master
inequality. The method must keep their common source variables; independent
worst-case copies destroy precisely the correlation the finite-word analysis is intended to retain.
The optional bias-separation sector is used only when proved, not assumed from
a source-family name.

The original vector-information, integrated-OU process-excitation and Riccati
lemmas establish linear ingredients under their stated window and covariance
hypotheses. They motivate useful storage candidates and numerical reduction.
They do not themselves establish finite-angle correction dissipation,
source-complete reset composition, or startup capture. Each proposed lemma
must be attached to a specific term of the complete-word inequality; compiling
more lemmas is not equivalent to closing that inequality.

\subsection{Compatible storage and a qualitative coercivity lemma}
The prescribed the finite-word analysis candidate is
\begin{equation}
 V(z,P)=z^{\top}M(P)z,\qquad M(P)=\operatorname{diag}(P_{21}^{-1},I_3).
 \label{eq:study-storage}
\end{equation}
The inverse is a mathematical real object, not an instruction to invert $P$
in the firmware. The full carried covariance is used in both modes. Before
this law can prove a state bound, its energy must be comparable to error size
with positive constants that hold throughout the relevant tail.

On any compact covariance domain satisfying
$0<\underline p I\preceq P\preceq\overline p I$, the storage is coercive:
there exist $0<m\le M<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
 m\|z\|^2\le V(z,P)\le M\|z\|^2 .
\end{equation}
The stability proof must establish forward invariance of such a covariance
domain; coercivity cannot be inferred from finite numerical tests alone.

This condition does not remove the ungauged observability obstruction:
covariance evolution alone cannot establish contraction of an unobservable
physical error.

For a legitimate quotient with orthonormal complementary frames $Q,N$, its
metric is minimum energy along the fibre,
\begin{equation}
 M_q=Q^{\top}MQ-Q^{\top}MN(N^{\top}MN)^{-1}N^{\top}MQ .
\end{equation}
This follows by minimizing $(Qy+N\eta)^{\top}M(Qy+N\eta)$ over $\eta$.
It is not generally the principal submatrix $Q^{\top}MQ$. The same qualitative
coercivity bounds follow from orthonormality and the minimization, provided
the physical quotient itself is justified.
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-binary32-coercivity.md}

\section{The master inequality and the conclusion it would justify}
\label{sec:study-comparison}

On the declared informative-service tail, the required finite-error estimate
has the form
\begin{equation}
 V_{j+1}\le\rho V_j+cD^2,\qquad 0\le\rho<1 .
 \label{eq:study-master}
\end{equation}
$D$ bounds the explicitly charged physical/model, bias and machine/sensor
supplies. The six canonical bias coordinates retain their actual dynamics
and justified bounds; they are not arbitrary independent noise. Zero sensor
noise alone need not give $D=0$. The constants in a uniform theorem must work
for all admitted histories in its stated regime; in the weaker history-wise
theorem they may depend on the history but must stay fixed along its tail.

The word inequality needs both coercivity and every-prefix transport:
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 m\|e_j\|^2&\le V_j\le M\|z_j\|^2,\qquad m>0,\\
 m\|e(t)\|^2&\le W(t)\le K V_j+c_pD^2,
 &t&\in[t_j,t_{j+1}).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:study-prefix}
\end{equation}
Here $e$ denotes the errors for which the conclusion is stated, with the
appropriate quotient/centre treatment in an ungauged result. Endpoints alone
do not control a large intermediate reset or an arithmetic failure.

\begin{theorem}[Conditional tail comparison; not a shipping certificate]
\label{thm:study-tail-comparison}
Suppose actual finite capture into a predeclared domain is proved or explicitly
assumed. Suppose the same carried trajectory is forward complete in that
domain, $t_j\to\infty$, and \eqref{eq:study-master}--\eqref{eq:study-prefix}
hold on every tail word and prefix with finite constants and bounded total
supply. Then
\begin{align}
 V_j&\le\rho^j V_0+cD^2\frac{1-\rho^j}{1-\rho},\\
 \limsup_{t\to\infty}\|e(t)\|^2
 &\le\frac{Kc/(1-\rho)+c_p}{m}D^2 .
 \label{eq:study-ultimate}
\end{align}
In the zero-total-supply case, the stated error converges to zero. If the
pre-capture prefix is bounded, the whole trajectory is bounded. No uniform
capture time follows from a history-dependent capture premise.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Repeated substitution in \eqref{eq:study-master} gives the geometric sum.
Since $\rho^j\to0$, apply the prefix bound, divide by $m$, and use
$t_j\to\infty$. A bounded finite prefix joined to the bounded tail gives the
whole-history bound. The argument uses neither the value nor a common maximum
of the capture time.
\end{proof}

Retention is not a consequence of an inequality that is valid only while the
trajectory stays inside a domain. For a domain containing an entire storage
sublevel, an endpoint check is
\begin{equation}
 V_0\le R,\qquad cD^2\le(1-\rho)R .
\end{equation}
It preserves the sublevel at endpoints. All intermediate domains, hybrid
jumps, chart choices and machine operations still need separate prefix checks.
This is how the proof avoids applying a local estimate after leaving its own
validity region. Useful ultimate bounds require useful $m,c,K,c_p$, not merely
a formal existence result with enormous constants.

\subsection{Genuinely nonuniform factors need more than $\rho_j<1$}
For $V_{j+1}\le r_jV_j+q_j$, exact substitution gives
\begin{equation}
 V_n\le\Pi(n,0)V_0+\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\Pi(n,k+1)q_k,
 \qquad \Pi(n,k)=\prod_{i=k}^{n-1}r_i .
\end{equation}
The initial product must decay and the disturbance convolution must remain
bounded (or a stronger decrement-scaled supply must be proved). For example,
$r_j=1-1/(j+2)^2$ is strictly below one at every step but
$\Pi(n,0)=(n+2)/(2n+2)\to1/2$. Thus individually favorable factors do not even
ensure forgetting the initial error. A finite collection of point ratios is
still weaker. The history-wise comparison theorem is useful precisely because
it states the necessary tail premises separately from capture.
\studysource{docs/ou3-alt-nonuniform-capture-theorem.md}
